<div class="wp-block-group alignwide article-body wrap"><!-- wp:group {"tagName":"aside","layout":{"type":"default"}} --><aside class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>FIELD NOTE / 021</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Distance / 46 km<br>Elevation / 1,480 m<br>Days / 03</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></aside><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {"className":"prose","layout":{"type":"default"}} --><div class="wp-block-group prose"><!-- wp:paragraph {"dropCap":true,"className":"dropcap"} --><p class="has-drop-cap dropcap">The road leaves the valley without ceremony. One moment there are houses, bright signs and tricycles moving between market stalls; the next, everything narrows to wet asphalt and pine.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>By six in the morning, cloud had erased the upper slopes. We drove with the windows open, listening for water in the gullies and the low mechanical complaint of buses climbing somewhere ahead.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">Before the weather turns</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every bend rearranged the same elements: rock, road, river and a horizon that appeared only briefly. At a roadside store above Atok, the owner set down three cups of coffee and pointed to the ridge.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p>“The mountain is not hiding. It is asking you to wait.”</p></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>So we waited. Rain ticked against the roof, a dog slept beneath a bench, and slowly the valley returned.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
